4. The critical distinction: objective evolution vs solver evolution

This is the point I would make central to the design.

Suppose:

P_t:
\quad
\min_x f_t(x)
\quad
\text{s.t. }Ax=b,\;x\in K.

There are two kinds of evolution.

Problem state

f_t,\quad A_t,\quad b_t,\quad K_t.

Algorithm state

x_t,\quad z_t,\quad y_t,\quad
\text{factorizations},\quad
\text{preconditioners},\ldots

A conventional solver mostly throws away the second after obtaining x_t.

Your proposed solver would explicitly preserve it:

\boxed{
(P_t,S_t)
\longrightarrow
(P_{t+1},S_{t+1}).
}

This is the key abstraction.

And it is exactly why I would put this into a standalone C library rather than bury it in skfolio.

