9. CVXPY is actually a very good frontend

This part is technically plausible.

CVXPY already supports custom solver interfaces. Its current documentation explicitly allows implementing a solver derived from QpSolver or ConicSolver, and CVXPY passes canonicalized numerical problem data to that interface. 

It also exposes canonicalized problem data through get_problem_data(), which can be passed directly to a solver. 

So conceptually:

x = cp.Variable(n)
Sigma = cp.Parameter((n, n), PSD=True)
mu = cp.Parameter(n)
problem = cp.Problem(
    cp.Minimize(
        cp.quad_form(x, Sigma) - mu @ x
    ),
    constraints=[
        cp.sum(x) == 1,
        x >= 0,
    ],
)
solver = cp.OnlineSolver(...)
problem.solve(solver=solver)

is compatible with the extension mechanism.

But there is an important caveat:

CVXPY’s normal .solve() abstraction is fundamentally a one-shot solve interface. Its custom solver mechanism is designed around giving a solver canonical problem data and receiving a solution. 

Therefore I would not force the entire online semantics into:

problem.solve(solver=cp.OnlineSolver)

alone.

