16. And it gives you a genuinely compelling story

I would position the standalone project approximately as:

A stateful convex optimization engine for sequential and online problems.

Traditional convex optimization treats each problem instance independently. In many real workloads, however, optimization problems arrive as a stream and successive instances differ only slightly. This library exploits temporal structure through persistent primal-dual state, warm starts, factorization reuse, continuation, and online optimization algorithms.

Then the examples become extremely compelling:

# classical
problem.solve()
# sequential
solver.solve_sequence(parameters)
# online
solver.online(loss_stream)
# portfolio
MeanRisk(..., solver="ONLINE")
# OCO
FollowTheWinner(strategy="eg", solver="ONLINE")

And the conceptual diagram is:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{ccccc}
\text{batch} &
\longrightarrow &
\text{warm-started} &
\longrightarrow &
\text{continuation}
\\
&&&&\downarrow\\
&&&&
\text{online convex optimization}
\end{array}}

with the same C numerical state underneath.

My recommendation

I would seriously pursue this as a standalone library rather than making skfolio-accelerate progressively more complicated.

The projects then acquire very clean responsibilities:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{lll}
\textbf{skfolio} &
:&
\text{high-level portfolio modeling + sklearn semantics}\\[2mm]
\textbf{skfolio universal\_portfolio} &
